Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> I'm still chewing over the data you selected on the given link and what
> the valid conclusions are that one can draw.

The data you used for your conclusions was a subset of mine.  Did you
consider your own conclusions valid?

> If I understand correct, this is all about difficulty compared between
> regular ascenders ("serial") and occasional ascenders ("non serial")?
> I.e., generally about the ascenders.

Well, in retrospect, the only effective means of measuring skill
currently is by "the ascension".  There is also "the conduct", but that
is self-selected and not stored in the standard nethack logfile, so not
available for us to peruse.

So, this analysis focused on "the ascension", and by some extension,
"the lack of" -- ie. someone who has not ascended at all.

>> This was prompted by the "best class" thread, where a number of
>> questions were raised regarding how to interpret data gained from
>> nethack.alt.org regarding games played, ascension percentages, etc.
>> [...]
>> [...] However, data garnered from there has a few obvious flaws.
>>
>> First, the data is a self-selecting pool.  People choose based on their
>> own personal reasons which class to play and how often; [...]
> 
> Can't see how you would have overcome this in your analysis; which
> seems just more detailled data in the highend player range but not
> considering that selection behaviour?

Well, it's not possible to completely overcome it.  What one can do is
what I did, which is minimize the effects.  The "unique games" and
"unique ascensions" table does a great deal of this -- a person who only
plays Wizards and ascends 17 of them will clearly skew the ascension
totals for Wizards, when realistically all we need to know is that he
can, in fact, ascend a Wizard.  The number of times he repeats his
success?  Not so useful.

>> [quitscumming]
> 
> With reference to XyyZy's response in this thread; getting better
> starting stats and objects will likely increase success just in the
> early game, the survivability of the buffed up classes (and whatever
> classes you play) blurs later in the game (and until ascension),
> compared to non quit-scummed games.

Not significantly; see responses to xyzzy and Link.

>> The data used in this analysis was taken from nethack.alt.org's logfile
>> on the morning of Sunday, February 25, 2007. 
> 
> Is that data downloadable anywhere? (I'd be interested!)

If you contact paxed/dtype, the admins of alt.org, they will probably be
able to help you get that stuff.

>> - -- Finally, there are two columns at the end named AAPF and AAPF/NS.
>> These were added upon the sudden realization that there is one major
>> truth which can wreck our data completely; that is, _the first ascension
>> is the hardest, regardless of class._
> 
> [I may misunderstand what you try to express by that statement, so I
> skip it at the moment. But at least to know about your assumptions...]
> 
> Are you assuming here that the first ascension has been achieved on NAO?

Nope.  Can't assume that, because we have no record and no means of
obtaining any information about that.  But it is still a truth, and thus
one we should at least attempt to measure.

>> Think about that one again.  The first ascension is ALWAYS the hardest.
> 
> (My _very first_ ascension took me 3 years of playing; it was a Knight!
> The second ascension was shortly after, a few months later; a Tourist!

See?  :)

> While true this seem irrelevant. What would be interesting for a "class
> difficulty" analysis would be the overwhelming amount of all the other
> deaths that are not a consequence of ignorance ("not knowing"), but are
> depending on the classes proficiencies, stats, etc., and in the mid game
> maybe also including the quest.

How do you classify deaths that are a consequence of ignorance compared
to deaths that are a consequence of laziness?  Both indicate a lack of
skill, but the latter will occur far more frequently -- deaths from
fountain abuse, for example.

>> Rough conclusions drawn:
>>
>> - -- Wizards really are the most common ascension on NAO, no matter how
>> you slice it.  59 more distinct players ascended a Wizard than the next
>> most common, Valkyrie.  29 more players have a Wizard as their "first".
> 
> (Isn't that another visible selection effect?)

Janis, I have to point out here that when I originally responded to your
post and mentioned that the data was self-selected and not random, and
therefore was suspect, that you were firmly on the side of "But of
course it is random!"

Now, however, you seem to be reversing your position.  Is it because my
analysis came back and proved that my "common sense" made more sense
than blindly trusting the raw data?

As I said before, I am familiar with the phenomenon of selective
perception.  But it works both ways -- in blindly trusting the raw data
without asking how that data was obtained, and in applying common sense
without even checking raw data in the first place.

This analysis did both.  Now, to answer your question:

Certainly the size of the jump could be at least partially due to a
selection effect.  But Valkyrie is _three times_ the size of Barbarian,
the third most common role.  Wizard is _four times_ the size of
Barbarian.  Self-selection is not going to create that much difference
over such a huge dataset... and especially not when you remove multiple
ascensions from the mix.

>> - -- Some classes are clearly ascended far more often by "less
>> experienced" players; comparing our top and bottom ascension counts for
>> "at least one ascension" and "two or more ascensions",  [...]
> 
> (So this is just covering the ascenders? I get the impression that
> your working hypothesis was; class difficulty is best derivable from
> the ascender statistics.

Yes. Do you believe you have a more accurate method of measuring skill?

Score isn't it, we both know.  Neither is the level on which death
occurred, as Dig For Victory and other fast-dive, corner-case high-risk
strategies will put you far, far down in the heap.  Plus, someone who
went down and did Sokoban and then died in Minetown will show too far
up, when they actually got rather farther than a newbie in the game.

>> and, of course, the 1st Ascension column:
>> Wiz, Val, Bar, Tou, Sam, Mon, Arc, Pri, Rog, Ran, Hea, Kni, Cav
> 
> I think this one is interesting. But how isn't that depending on the
> selection process?

Because most honest-to-god _new_ players try a bunch of different
classes until they find one they achieve success with.  The one they
most often achieved success with is, thus, very likely to be the easiest
one.

Even someone coming to NAO after primarily playing locally will pick the
class they consider 'easiest' to ascend first, in order to get off the
schneid so they don't get smacked with !asc every time they try to offer
advice on #nethack.  So the selection process actually could be
considered to help us there, by some lights... but I prefer to consider
it a wash.

>> - -- Caveman and Knight are unpopular roles; a player doesn't tend to
>> ascend them until they have several ascensions under their belt.
> 
> I can't assume that this is the case. (But who knows what "a player"
> does and dosen't...?)

You don't have to assume.  The data says that on average, non-serial
players ascend more than THREE TIMES prior to ascending their first
Caveman or Knight.  Include the serial players and it jumps even higher;
a clear indication that "serial" ascenders only play those roles to get
them in the "list of 13", and some probably not even then.

After three ascensions, a player is not even close to 'newbie' anymore.

Unpopular?  Well, you can tell that just by looking at how few games are
played by those two compared to the rest of the server.

> I must assume that, WRT pre-selection of roles, there are different
> types of players, for example...
> - players that start with a role they feel to be easy because they
>   don't die very often in the first few levels (like Valkyries and
>   Barbarians),

Making Cavemen and Knights unpopular.

> - players that select roles because of their interest in the class
>   (you named it Monk-and-Ninja disease?, but also Wizards are very
>   popular RPG classes, and there are also people that select the
>   somewhat "strange" classes like Tourists just out of interest),

Making Cavemen and Knights unpopular.

> - players that play Nethack for a long time and want to play a new
>   available class (like the Monk and Ranger that haven't been part
>   of Nethack in earlier versions),

Making Cavemen and Knights unpopular.

> - players that just select the classes they haven't yet ascended,

Meaningless; almost nobody has ascended a Caveman.  This would actually
make Cavemen or Knights MORE popular since they are the least-ascended
classes.

> - players that select classes randomly, whether they ascended or not.

A perfect sample for our purposes.

>   "I'm new to nethack and I am addicted to it. Does anyone know
>    what the all around best class is?"
> 
> I don't think that this is covered at all by the current data. 

Au contraire.  It's covered excellently by the current data.  The best
class for a brand new player _if they want to ascend_ is Valkyrie,
followed very closely by Wizard.

Which, of course, we all knew anyway.  Common sense, see.

> [**] The factor of startscumming seems to be not a problem for most
> classes, but just for the classes that allow significant variations
> at the beginning (and it balances out quite fast anyway). For the
> "best class" question in this _early game context_ the numbers for,
> e.g., Wizards and especially Rogues must be taken with some care.

Did you notice where I filtered out all "quits" and "escapes" in the
bottom table, yes?

Xyzzy's and Link's questions were valid -- but the low total ascension
numbers achieved by the quitscummers indicate that again, it isn't
particularly helpful to quitscum if you don't know how to ascend in the
first place.

